/** Translation maps for siman 041 — imported by _patch-siman-041.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `The laws of liver treifot. In it are 10 seifim. If the liver was removed — treifah; unless there remained an olive-volume in the place of the gall and an olive-volume in the place of its vitality — which is where it depends on it — and these two olive-volumes are each whole in its place; but if it was gathered together, or like a strap, or flattened — treifah.`,
    '2#main': `This measure is like two olive-volumes even in a large ox and in fowl — everything according to its size, large and small (see above siman 20 seif 2 and siman 34 seif 4).`,
    '3#main': `If the liver dried until it crumbles under a fingernail — treifah; and this is when all of it dried and there did not remain an olive-volume in the place of the gall and an olive-volume in the place of its vitality. {Rama: And if these two olive-volumes alone dried — also treifah. Melting of the liver — that is, blood comes out from the flesh of the liver that melted and became blood — treifah (Rokeach); even though the two olive-volumes remained, for in the end it will rot — all (his opinion). There are those who say: a liver that is hard as a stone — treifah (Rokeach and Avraham ben David and Darkei Moshe); and this is when the two aforementioned olive-volumes did not remain, that they did not harden.}`,
    '4#main': `If worms infested the liver — even in the place of the gall and in the place of its vitality — kosher.`,
    '5#main': `If the liver was uprooted in many places and exposed in its omasum here a little and there a little — kosher.`,
    '6#main': `A needle found in the liver — whether it came before us whole or cut — if the thick side of it is toward the cavity of the animal, even if all of it is buried in the flesh of the liver — treifah; and if the thin side of it faces outside — kosher, for we attribute and say it came via the simfon. {Rama: And specifically when the thick side faces toward the simfon; but if it faces to the sides — in every case treifah (Beit Yosef).} In what case is this said? With a thick needle; but with a thin one there is no distinction and it is treifah; and the measure of thick is like the measure of a date pit.`,
    '7#main': `A needle found in a large simfon of the liver — which is the reed-simfon that enters into it; and likewise if found in a large simfon of the lung — kosher.`,
    '8#main': `If the omasum of the liver was punctured with a puncture open through from side to side from the side of the liver — treifah; but if it adhered with an adhesion on a rib — kosher. {Rama: And if the omasum of the liver adhered to the abomasum — one must examine the rumen, for certainly there is a thorn or needle that punctured and on account of this this adhesion came (thus I found in old examinations); and I saw an incident and so is the practice.}`,
    '9#main': `If a needle is found in the omasum — doubtful treifah.`,
    '10#main': `If two livers are found in an animal or in fowl — treifah; for any excess is considered as removed, and it is as though the liver was removed.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Mishnah Chullin daf 42.`,
    '2#_': `Mishnah daf 54.`,
    '3#_': `Like Rabbi Zeira.`,
    '4#_': `Like Rav Ada bar Ahavah and Rav Papa, who require both.`,
    '5#_': `Tur; and so Rashba in Tosafot HaRishonim and Ran.`,
    '6#_': `Rosh regarding lung that dried, in name of Bahag and Raavad.`,
    '7#_': `Tur and Rashba in Tosafot.`,
    '8#_': `Baraisa there daf 48.`,
    '9#_': `Tur and Rashba in Tosafot.`,
    '10#_': `A question there in the Gemara daf 46, and it was resolved.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Unless, etc. There 43:1 — law as stated.`,
    '1#ב': `In the place, etc. There 46:1.`,
    '1#ג': `That it depends. Per the first explanation in Rashi; and so Rashba and Tur.`,
    '1#ד': `But if, etc. There is an unresolved question that was not clarified.`,
    '2#_': `This measure, etc. See Tosafot there 44:1 s.v. kedei; and Rivam explains, etc.`,
    '3#א': `Dry, etc. From the law of lung that dried, as implies there that it is as though it does not exist; therefore even part of it. There.`,
    '3#ב': `Until it crumbles, etc. As written regarding lung. There. (And if they dried, etc. — see above siman 44 s.k. 10.)`,
    '3#ג': `Melting, etc. even though, etc. As written in Gittin 69a.`,
    '4#_': `Even, etc. For otherwise why did they not permit until the lower third of the leg; and it is worse than removed. (Ran).`,
    '6#א': `If, etc. even, etc. Tosafot there s.v. iy — see there.`,
    '6#ב': `And specifically, etc. For via the simfon it cannot bend. Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe.`,
    '6#ג': `In what case is this said, etc. Gemara there.`,
    '6#ד': `And the measure, etc. For it is impossible for experts to know which is thick; and at this measure certainly it cannot puncture, as written siman 59:1. Tosafot.`,
    '7#_': `Needle, etc. and likewise, etc. Gemara there; and they did not distinguish between kofa, etc., for certainly the simfon took it and it came, as written in the Gemara.`,
    '8#_': `(Likkut) If the omasum was punctured, etc. For in the end the entire liver will be removed, and we forbid on account of the end; and as they forbade there siman 56:2 "became yellow" liver, etc. Mordechai siman 499; and Hagahot Aharon siman 49 s.v. and the measure of its yellowness, etc. — see there (end).`,
    '9#_': `Found, etc. As with found in the cavity of the body — Peri Megadim.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(At end of Taz s.k. 2) His words are correct for practical law, but he elaborated unnecessarily; and this is already explained briefly in Shach s.k. 4. Also what he challenged the Bach from "became yellow" liver opposite the intestines is nothing; and this is simple.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 41 seif 2) This measure that is like two olive-volumes even in a large ox and in fowl everything according to its size and smallness — this is the language of Rashba in Tosafot. And behold that which he cites "even in a large ox" and in fowl everything, etc. — implies that he holds the measures stated in the mishnah apply even in a small animal and one does not estimate at all according to size, only in fowl; and what compelled him to this is according to Rif's explanation in fowl: what does it not refer to — puncture like a wing. And one may challenge: if so, why does he explain more in fowl than in all other animals? Perforce one estimates the large according to its size, etc.; if so, the same in fowl. And Rashba, to resolve this difficulty, derived from this that all these measures that we learned in the mishnah in an animal — every animal is included, even, etc.; but in a small animal, since it states anonymously, it implies it refers to every animal; therefore he asks in fowl what one estimates. And he answered: accordingly, behold regarding removal of the crop he himself wrote Rashba and one estimates in a small one according to its smallness; and one must say: since the Gemara stated explicitly "like the fill of an ox's shoe" — it implies specifically the measure is so in an ox.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 41 seif 1) In the place of the gall. And in species that have no gall, as below (siman 42) — it suffices if there remained only an olive-volume in the place of its vitality.`,
    '2#_': `(There) And an olive-volume in the place of its being. And if there remained an olive-volume only in the place of the gall or only in the place of its vitality — treifah from doubt.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 3 in the hagahah) And if they dried. The language of Rashba in Tosafot: dried — an olive-volume in the place of the gall or in the place of its vitality — treifah. And so it appears from that which is below (siman 52 seif 1).`,
    '4#_': `(There) It was not explained whether this is specifically when the entire olive dried, but something of it remained that did not dry — kosher, since the rest of the liver exists and also something of this olive; or whether one requires specifically that the entire olive exist, and if something of it dried — treifah. Afterward I saw Peri Megadim was uncertain about this. And it appears to me to infer from what Taz wrote s.k. 2: the intent of Rashba in that which he wrote the law in "they dried" two olive-volumes and did not cite the law in "removed" the two olive-volumes — he goes according to his view that removed liver means also the sinews and simfonot; if so, in removal of the two olive-volumes also the sinews and simfonot — it is obvious treifah, since all its hanging was removed, the entire liver was removed anyway; and likewise in the place of the gall the gall was removed — see there. It is clear he holds it is not treifah except when the entire olive was removed and nothing remained; for if removed — all the more so treifah — you find the law well in removal. Also one may infer from s.k. 6 that so is Kereti's view from what he wrote in this law "they dried" two olive-volumes — they are stringent that an olive is a third of an egg; and if we say one requires the entire olive to exist, and automatically half an egg is a stringency — all the more so if something remained it is kosher; and on this we say stringently that one requires something to remain of that third of egg — that is called an olive; but if the third of egg dried and afterward something remained — the entire olive does not help. And this is clear. Further, I still have a place of doubt in the foundation of an olive in the place of the gall: if the thickness of the place of the gall is two olive-volumes, and in the place where the gall adheres the thickness of an olive was ruined, but nevertheless in thickness an olive remained intact from outside — if this is called "there remained an olive in the place of the gall," for all the thickness is called the place of the gall; or whether one requires the olive that is adjacent to the gall specifically. And from Kereti's words above one may infer that specifically in the very place of the gall is required; for if all the thickness is called place of the gall — what stringency is there in the matter that we judge an olive as a third of an egg, namely as we explained that one requires something to remain of that third; behold all thickness is called place of the gall and something from anywhere in the thickness helps; perforce the very place of the gall is required adjacent to it; and this requires study. And nevertheless for practical law there is doubt.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#_': `That it depends on it. In the kidneys, as stated in Beit Yosef and poskim; and there are poskim who say place of its vitality means the place of its adhesion in the omasum; and Orach Chaim wrote a general rule 51: the law is eight — therefore three olive-volumes are required in these three places — end of his words; and he brings it in Tosafot Chadashim general rule 89 law 9 to decide practical law.`,
    '3#א': `Until it crumbles under a fingernail. And if one scratches with a fingernail on the flesh of the liver and the scratch is visible inside it — it is called dry and treifah, as above siman 36 seif 13 in the hagahah.`,
    '3#ב': `And if these olive-volumes dried, etc. Thus is implied in Tur; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 17; and so Orach Chaim there; and Taz hung this law on "it appears to me" as though he judges by sight; however Maharshal there challenged Tur and wrote: and these are his words — I do not know from where he took this, for from that which we ask (Shas daf 55b) regarding flayed: removed like a sela on the spine and at the ends of the limbs and the rest remains — what is the law (see above siman 59 regarding flayed) — we do not ask thus regarding liver; it implies in liver it is kosher; and so Rashba and Ran wrote only that if the liver dried and the two olive-volumes did not remain; but in any case if the entire liver exists or most of it and those two olive-volumes were removed or dried — kosher, as stated below for practical law and not for action — end of his words; and he did not intend according to law what he wrote "I do not know," etc. — the Rambam slipped his mind, chapter 8 of Hilchot Shechitah law 21, who wrote: removed from it the place on which it depends and the place of the gall even though the rest remains — as though removed — treifah — end of his words; and so is everyone; and so is proven explicitly from Rashba's words in the long Tosafot 39b; and in brief there regarding two livers adhered with an olive in the place of the gall and an olive in the place of its vitality — treifah — see his name; and Beit Yosef brings it; and Maharshal there siman 19; and so is proven from Rashba's words that Tur brought siman 52 regarding liver became yellow opposite place of gall and opposite place of its vitality; and as appears from Maharshal's own words there siman 94; and that which the Gemara does not ask thus regarding liver — this is because in liver it is obvious to forbid, since the essence of its vitality was removed; and all this is clear.`,
    '3#ג': `Melting of the liver, etc. And in the Gemara in Gittin (daf 69a) it appears that it is not treifah except when the two olive-volumes did not remain, as with removal; and it appears to me that so is Rokeach's view; and so in responsum Masat Binyamin siman 21 — see his name; and so Bach agreed for practical law; and so in Sefer Tosafot Chadashim daf 134a; and in Lechem Mishneh daf 68b; and Bach followed Rabbenu Yitzchak; and in my humble opinion, as written.`,
    '3#ד': `There are those who say: liver, etc. And in Sefer Api Ravrevi he wrote: in great financial loss or at time of need one may be lenient in this.`,
    '4#_': `Even in the place of the gall, etc. Bach wrote: even if the entire liver was removed and only these olive-volumes remained and these that remained became wormy — kosher — see his name. And so is implied explicitly in Tosafot Chadashim general rule 89 law 9 — see his name and in the simanim. And Maharshal's view in Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 42 does not appear so, for he wrote: that which is kosher when wormy in place of gall and place of its vitality — this is when between all of them there is only the punctures of the worms like an olive-volume of flesh in place of its vitality and in place of the gall; and if not, it is obvious one may say the Gemara teaches us that since it became so wormy, in the end it will become wormy and spoil entirely; and Ran leans to be stringent per this reasoning — and this is the essence — end. And if so, when all was removed and the two olive-volumes that remained became wormy — there are not here two olive-volumes alone but worm punctures — and treifah; and so is Ra'ah's view in Sefer Beit Hashem; and perhaps also Tosafot Chadashim and Bach do not deal except when it became wormy in a case where the two olive-volumes were not lacking from their measure; and this requires study.`,
    '6#א': `Even if all of it is buried in the flesh of the liver — treifah. For we are concerned lest it entered via the esophagus to the thin parts and exited from the thin parts to the cavity of the animal and punctured, and from there entered the liver. Tur's language.`,
    '6#ב': `Facing outside. And Atzei Tzvi wrote: even if it is not buried in the flesh of the liver but only its point goes out into the cavity of the animal and its thick head is hidden inside the liver — kosher — end; and he followed Rashba's words that Beit Yosef brought, who reconciles Rashi's view and distinguishes: although we hold below siman 51, a thorn found in the cavity of the body is treifah lest perhaps one of the inner organs was punctured — that is specifically there where it punctured with force; but with a needle that entered gently we are not concerned for this. But Ran in Piskei Avodah Zarah concludes: where the needle goes out to the cavity of the body — treifah; and so Maharshal ruled Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 47; however in Semak and Orach Chaim general rule 51 law 17 it is written like Rashba; and so in Hagahot Shaarei Dura siman 94; and see there s.k. 3 and siman 55 what he wrote from this.`,
    '6#ג': `Kosher, etc. And Rashba holds: if its point faces toward the cavity of the body — treifah, for we are concerned for anything that entered until the place of the liver and its point downward, and it passes via a narrow path lest with its entrance it punctured the reed branching to the liver in the place where it is narrow — treifah, as written at end of siman 34; and he wrote that so is Rif's view; and so Maharshal and Bach.`,
    '6#ד': `In every case — treifah. For if it entered via the simfon it would not have been possible for it to bend entirely and lodge in the thickness of the liver — Beit Yosef; and what he wrote in Derishah siman 12 — see his name — Rabbenu Yerucham in this is an error, for on the contrary in Darkei Moshe he agreed to Beit Yosef, as written in the hagahah here.`,
    '6#ה': `But with a thin one there is no distinction. Whether the thick side of it faces the reed or toward the cavity of the animal — always treifah, even if all of it is inside the liver, for it is fit to puncture on its thick side; and if so, one must be concerned it entered via the esophagus; and see Tosafot and poskim who distinguished: why do we not distinguish above at end of siman 36 regarding lung between kofa inward or outward, that the simfonot of the lung if wide — see his name. (The law of the needle was explained in siman 48 and 49 and 51.)`,
    '8#א': `If the omasum was punctured, etc. And Darkei Moshe wrote in name of Maharil: if the courtyard of the liver was punctured in the place where the butcher's hand rests — kosher; and if white threads like milk are found around it — then it is an old puncture and treifah — end; and see above siman 36 s.k. 14.`,
    '8#ב': `From the side of the liver, etc. For the omasum is wider than the liver, and on the right side of the omasum it is not covered by the liver, and there the puncture can be far from the liver — end; Rambam in Semag; and so in responsum Divrei Rivash siman 173: specifically from the side of the liver is treifah; but Bach proved that this "from the side of the liver" is a scribal error, for it was written "courtyard of the liver" and they erred and inserted "from the side" in place of "courtyard"; and so he emended in Gedulat Mordechai; and so it is in the new slaughterings printed in Lublin year 534 in name of Maharal of Prague and in name of several great ones; and so I found explicitly in Agur siman 1032.`,
    '8#ג': `But if, etc. The reason the poskim wrote: treifot is not from itself but because of the puncture, in the end the liver will be removed; and so it appears to me to prove from the Gemara Piskei Avodah Zarah (daf 48a) that courtyard of the liver is not treifah from itself — see his name; and examine well. And according to this, whether a membrane grew on it from a blow or other membrane — it is a sealing; and so in responsum Divrei Rivash there: when the puncture was sealed with fat — kosher; and this is not like Ratz in examinations; and Rambam already challenged him in Sefer Beit Hashem.`,
    '9#_': `Needle in the omasum. Even if all of it is hidden in the omasum — on account of concern lest one of the inner organs was punctured upon their entrance there; and there is no distinction in the omasum between kofa inward or outward or outward, as Bach wrote; and see above siman 40 seif 3.`,
    '10#_': `For any excess is considered as removed. This implies even if they are not adhered to one another in place of its vitality and in place of the gall — for any excess is considered as removed, meaning as though both were removed entirely; therefore wherever two livers are found — treifah; and so wrote Tosafot Chadashim general rule 89 law 11; and so Maharshal ruled Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 18. However Maharshal wrote there: if the two livers begin below that olive in place of gall and in place of its vitality — it is obvious it is kosher according to everyone, like excess of the leg that goes out below the knee (below siman 55 seif 4) — end; and one must say it is worse if excess of liver is found that is not adhered at all to the liver than if adhered below that olive, for since it begins below — from the place of its vitality and place of the gall it appears it is only liver tendrils; and this requires study; and see at end of siman 43 and siman 50 and 54 and 55 what I wrote from this.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `That it depends on it. In the Gemara we say it is alive, and Rashi explains: namely the place where it was stuck under the kidneys; and a second language — excess of the liver — and these are the omasum; and the poskim agreed on it that the essence is according to the first language; and in Tosafot Chadashim he brings in name of Orach Chaim to be concerned also for the second language.`,
    '3#_': `Melting of the liver, etc. In this he followed Rokeach whom Beit Yosef brought, who learned it from chapter "One who seized him"; and the honor of Rama rests in its place, but he was not precise in this ruling; and I will set before you the Gemara's words that are stated there: for blood that comes from the mouth we examine it with a wheat stalk whether it absorbs; if the blood sticks to the stalk — lung disease comes and it has a remedy; if not — liver disease comes and it has no remedy. We challenge: but we learned the opposite — liver removed and nothing remained of it; lung punctured or lacking — treifah. We answer: since it comes from the mouth, say perhaps it melted and was removed; and Rashi explains: since blood of the liver, which hangs below the lung, rises upward and exits via the mouth — say perhaps it melted and was removed entirely and nothing remained of it, and it fell to the lung via the reed of the liver connected to the reed of the lung — end; it appears clearly the main ruin that the animal or person dies is that the entire liver melted, not part of it; for if there is a flaw also in part of it — the difficulty returns to its place, for a lung that melted and spoiled inside is kosher as long as the simfonot did not melt; if so, liver would be stricter than lung when lacking in part — but we learned the opposite as the questioner cited. Rather, simple: whoever did not lack the two olive-volumes is kosher even if the liver melted. And Rokeach wrote: reed of liver like liver in removal — all of it and nothing remained like an olive — treifah; liver that melted — treifah, as we say in "One who seized him": blood from the mouth — if from liver disease it has no remedy; liver that dried or is hard as stone — treifah — end. And in this Beit Yosef understood Rokeach forbids in melting even if the two olive-volumes remained intact, and he disagreed; and indeed Rokeach did not come to forbid except in melting entirely even the two olive-volumes; and a great novelty he teaches us: if melted — it is as removed; and so we find in Beit Yosef siman 50 in name of Rashba: any limb whose puncture or lack disqualifies — when melted also disqualifies — end; and so Mordechai whom Beit Yosef brought siman 50: Rabbenu Tam said to Ra'am mouth to mouth that wherever punctured forbids — likewise if the flesh around it is bad and rotted; and it itself teaches also Rokeach: even in liver that is all blood — nevertheless if it melted and became blood it forbids if the two olive-volumes did not remain; this is proven clearly from his words, for behold adjacent to this he wrote: liver that dried or is hard as stone — treifah, meaning even the two olive-volumes; and so Rama wrote here: if not so — kosher, as written in this seif; and why not explain the first clause thus too, namely what he wrote "liver that melted" — what difference to me liver that melted or dried? Rather it is clear and simple that Rokeach who forbids in melting means also in melting entirely; and this matter is a great wonder on Rama: in dried he required dryness in all of it, and likewise hard as stone, and in melting he forbids even if the two olive-volumes remained; and Rokeach wrote them adjacent one to another in one language; rather it is simple he refers to all of it in melting too; and Moharach came to strengthen Rama's ruling and said Rokeach disagrees with Rashi and explains another meaning: namely since it exits via the mouth certainly there is rot there and "throw the rot in all of it"; and it is difficult: if so, that which is stated Piskei Avodah Zarah (daf 56): Rav Yosef son of Rav Yehudah asked Rav Yehudah: liver became yellow opposite the intestines — what is the law? He said: treifah. We challenge: let it be only removed — kosher. Rava said: since liver became yellow opposite the intestines, it is known the intestines fell into fire and constricted; and if we say "throw rot in all of it" and in the end it will rot — he would not challenge at all; and also Rava would not need to make treifah on account of the intestines — let it be forbidden because we throw rot in all of the liver. Rather, certainly we do not say in liver "throw rot in all of it," aside from that it is already hidden in our words; for if so it would be stricter than lung — why not say also in lung we throw rot in all of it? Perforce we have no basis to be stringent in a matter Rashi explicitly permits; and to learn from Rokeach's words what he did not intend at all. And again I saw he wrote as our words in Masat Binyamin newly printed: we decide practical law and so we judge a long time practically every day that one does not forbid rotted and became blood if the two olive-volumes remained whole; and one should not be stringent at all in this except if it is known the animal fell from a height of ten handbreadths and crushing and loss of form in the liver is found — forbid, as written siman 58 seif 3.`,
    '4#_': `If worms infested the liver, etc. In Mishmeret HaBayit Rashba wrote: even if the entire liver was removed and only the two olive-volumes remained and these two olive-volumes became wormy — kosher, for the infestation returns healthy.`,
    '6#א': `Even if all of it is buried, etc. Say perhaps it came via the esophagus, punctured, and entered the liver; and Tosafot wrote: and it appears specifically regarding liver there is a distinction between kofa inward and outward, but lung is kosher in every case, for it is close to the reed and the simfon of the lung has space and a large cavity; and likewise in the reticulum it is kosher, for the way of the esophagus the needle comes with the food that hangs on the esophagus — end; and so Beit Yosef brought in name of Rashba in responsum siman 255: in liver they were stringent since it is far from the reed; therefore when the thin side enters toward the liver we are concerned it punctured upon its entrance, since it travels a long path and a narrow place; and we are concerned perhaps via its entrance it was here and there and punctured in the heart's simfonot to the liver, since it is a long path — end; per this it appears the same for a needle found in the spleen; and see what is written siman 43 seif 1.`,
    '6#ב': `In the flesh, etc. In Tur it is written we are concerned lest it entered via the esophagus to the thin parts, etc.; if it entered via the simfon, etc. — wondrous: for it implies from this that if we did not have this proof we would not forbid from doubt but would attribute that it entered via the reed; and afterward he wrote: but with a thin one there is no distinction and treifah in every case, etc. — it implies from doubt alone we forbid; and it appears we certainly forbid on account of doubt alone, since most swallowings are via the esophagus; but in the first clause he should have said we do not attribute that it entered via the esophagus since it has a pouch even though its point downward punctures well — nevertheless how can the pouch enter via there, which is a small puncture? Rather there was room to attribute (in the pouch) [in the reed]; perforce he said you must be stringent even though it is not reasonable except what entered via the reed — how did it puncture the flesh of the liver after exiting from the simfon? Thus the two sides are balanced; nevertheless treifah from doubt. And the explanation of the matters regarding kofa outward is thus: if found kofa outward — namely toward the cavity of the body and its point toward the simfon — then perforce it did not come via the simfon to the flesh but from outside, namely via the esophagus to the thin parts and from thin parts to liver; the law is: its point inside flesh of liver toward the simfon; but if kofa toward the simfon — there is proof it came specifically from the simfon; for if from outside it would not have been possible to enter with the pouch inside the liver; and we need not be concerned lest it came from outside from another side of the liver and there its point was in the flesh of the liver; for certainly it is reasonable that here it was found here, since it is possible to say it came here via simfon — it is more reasonable than to say it came from another side from outside to here; and Beit Yosef and Rama already wrote: since if placed straight in the liver — treifah, for then it is impossible it came via the simfon. And Rashba in the long Tosafot daf 36 wrote another explanation according to Rif and Rambam, and these are his words: and it appears they explain kofa outward opposite Rashi's words: and inward means opposite the cavity of the animal; and outward means opposite outside the cavity — namely opposite the head; and this principle is transmitted to us from the sages: whatever is found in lung or in one of the hanging organs — we say it took the simfon and came; and here this is what it says: if kofa inward — kosher, for it took the simfon and came, and we are not concerned it turned here and there and punctured; but when kofa outward and its sharp head enters inward — we are concerned lest it turned here and there via its descent and punctured the reed of the liver, whose puncture is slight, since it passed there in a narrow place; not so in lung, for until the lung the place is wide and close — we are not concerned at all, even kofa outward, etc.; but wondrous in liver too: if kofa inward and lodged in liver — why kosher? For if via simfon it came — how was it lodged? And I can say: whatever is inside — the power of attraction in the organs pulls and pushes — end; and it appears in Bahag that Tur brings siman 40 he explains here also per this explanation of Rashba; and why was he not concerned above lest it entered via esophagus? For we say more that it entered via simfon.`,
    '6#ג': `But if it faces to the sides — treifah. For certainly it entered via the esophagus; for if it entered via the simfon it would not have been possible to bend so much to the width of the liver — Beit Yosef.`,
    '7#_': `In a large simfon. Even if the liver is cut — so Maharshal; and likewise lung.`,
    '8#א': `From the side of the liver. So in Mordechai; and Moharach was astonished: here it implies there is a distinction that if punctured not from the side of the liver — kosher, and other poskim did not distinguish; and so in Tosafot siman 163 he brought Mordechai's words and did not mention "from the side of the liver"; also in Orach Chaim it implies there is no distinction — see his name; general rule 51 siman 34; therefore he wrote this "from the side" is a scribal error — it was written in Mordechai "courtyard of the liver" and the copyist erred and wrote "from the side" in place of "courtyard"; and it appears to me that which he wrote "from the side of the liver" does not come to exclude that it would be kosher in courtyard of the liver in any place on the surface of courtyard of the liver — for certainly everywhere treifah; rather his intent is: in courtyard of the liver there is a part spread on the platform of the animal, and afterward it begins to descend in the cavity of the animal via the break on the liver in the air of the cavity; and on this he said: whatever has a puncture in all the area inside the air of the cavity; but in that part spread on the wall — a puncture does not disqualify; and this is clear in his reason, for Beit Yosef wrote in name of Hagahot Ashiri: the reason for treifot in courtyard of the liver is not from itself but that in the end the liver will be removed; therefore adhesion does not disqualify — on the contrary, whatever was sealed helps; therefore also if there is a puncture open through in that part stuck to the wall — all the more there is no prohibition, for the wall covers the puncture even though it does not enter inside and is stuck to it — nevertheless air does not enter there via that puncture, for the wall covers the puncture; perforce kosher. Thus we find all poskim did well not to distinguish, for they spoke only of the part that is in the air of the animal; and Mordechai wanted to teach us not to err and say that even the part by the wall is called courtyard of the liver — as explained, clear and correct.`,
    '8#ב': `But if it adhered, etc. For since the puncture does not disqualify from itself, as Mordechai wrote Piskei Avodah Zarah, it turned to light — sealing via the adhesion helps; and perforce even impure milk that generally does not seal — here in the puncture of courtyard of the liver it seals; and so he wrote. And aside from this, adhesion does not forbid in courtyard of the liver, for we do not say there is no adhesion without a puncture except in lung that draws all kinds of liquids; so Orach Chaim general rule 62; and not like Terumat HaDeshen. Darkei Moshe wrote: and I found to Mahari Mullin: if courtyard of liver was punctured in place where butcher's hand rests — kosher; and if white threads like milk are found around it — then it is an old puncture and treifah — end.`,
    '9#_': `Needle found in omasum. Explanation: all covered by omasum; for otherwise it is obvious treifah, for it is no better than found in cavity of body. It appears to me the examiner need not check courtyard of liver whether it has a puncture or not, for we rely on majority like we need not check after other treifot except lung adhesions that are common; and even though I saw in some examinations to check afterward — it appears to me this is not obligatory; and a fortiori one should not punish the examiner for this as with other treifot.`,
    '10#_': `Two livers, etc. See what is written siman 55 seif 4: one does not forbid excess of liver except in place of gall or its vitality.`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `Depends. Namely in the kidneys, as stated in Beit Yosef; and there are poskim who say place of its vitality is the place of its adhesion in the omasum; and Orach Chaim wrote: therefore three olive-volumes are required in these three places; and he brings it in Tosafot Chadashim to decide practical law — end; Shach. However in fowl one may be lenient if two olive-volumes remained, one in place of gall and one in place where it adheres to the heart — see Shaarei Yosher siman 51. A goose liver for roasting was on the lower side resting on the esophagus and reticulum like a pocket of flesh covered with liver skin; it was tall and somewhat harder than liver flesh; he tore the pocket and found inside a complete foxtail, also rotted and digested, also sand like that found in reticulum — treifah. Responsum Shaarei De'ah question 28 and Peri Chadash in Kuntras A; and see Radbaz siman 199. A stone found in liver of animal buried in flesh of liver is fit, for there it forms from food matter descending to the liver to form blood. Peri Chadash in name of Rikanti.`,
    '3#א': `Under a fingernail. And Shach wrote: if one scratches with a fingernail on flesh of liver and the scratch is visible inside — it is called dry and treifah, as above siman 36 seif 13 in hagahah.`,
    '3#ב': `All. And Taz and Shach disagree and rule it is not treifah except when the two olive-volumes did not remain, as with removal. And Taz wrote: and so we judge a long time; and so in Masat Binyamin, except if it is known the animal fell ten handbreadths high and crushing and loss of form in liver is found — forbid, as written siman 58 seif 3 — end.`,
    '3#ג': `As a stone. And Shach wrote in name of Sefer Api Ravrevi: in great financial loss or time of need one may be lenient in this.`,
    '4#_': `Its vitality. And Bach ruled: even if entire liver removed and only these two olive-volumes that became wormy remained — nevertheless kosher, for infestation returns healthy. But Maharshal ruled in such a case treifah, and Shach agreed with him; and he wrote: perhaps also Bach does not deal except when it became wormy in a case where the two olive-volumes were not lacking from their measure.`,
    '6#א': `Buried. In Tur he wrote the reason for concern: lest it entered via esophagus to thin parts and from thin parts to cavity of animal, punctured, and entered liver. And Tosafot wrote: specifically regarding liver there is distinction between kofa inward or outward, but lung kosher in every case, for close to reed and simfon of lung has space; and likewise in reticulum kosher, for way of esophagus needle comes with food hanging on esophagus; and so Beit Yosef in name of Rashba: in liver they were stringent since far from reed — end; Taz; and see above siman 43 regarding needle in spleen — see his name. On night of Sukkot they found in liver pieces after they roasted and cooked a needle stuck in one middle piece of liver; needle was of those needles women use much; he declared kosher — Tzitz Eliezer siman 107 from force of doubtful case: perhaps after slaughter, perhaps before slaughter — perhaps via reed it entered and did not puncture any organ of those whose puncture is slight; especially since needle was of those women carry stuck on garment wings — perhaps after roasting it fell; see Radbaz siman 28. But Peri Chadash was stringent except vessels in which liver was cooked — one may be lenient to use them when not Beit Yosef, for it is as removed. If date pits found in liver — kosher; olive pits — treifah. Peri Chadash.`,
    '6#ב': `Outside. And Shach wrote in name of Ran and Maharshal: specifically if all buried in flesh of liver; but if its point goes into cavity of animal and thick head hidden in liver — treifah; and not like Atzei Tzvi and Semak and Orach Chaim and Hagahot Shaarei Dura who permit. Greater than this Rashba holds: even if only its point faces cavity of animal — also treifah, for we are concerned for anything that entered until place of liver and its point downward and passed narrow path lest with its entrance it punctured reed branching to liver where narrow — treifah; and so Maharshal and Bach.`,
    '6#ג': `Treifah. For if it entered via simfon it would not have been possible to bend entirely and lodge in thickness of liver — Shach in name of Beit Yosef.`,
    '6#ד': `Thin. For it is fit to puncture on its thick side. Shach. And our needles are law of thin needle — treifah in every case. So is practice in name of Hagahot Mordechai daf 155.`,
    '7#א': `In the liver. Even if liver is cut — Taz in name of Maharshal.`,
    '7#ב': `Lung. And even if it is cut. Taz.`,
    '8#א': `From the side. Behold almost all poskim write this "from the side of the liver" is a scribal error and must say courtyard of the liver; and Taz wants to resolve: meaning to exclude if punctured in that part spread on the wall — then puncture does not disqualify, for wall covers it — see his name; and his words are strained. (And I heard from Shulchan Aruch "from the side of the liver" means from the side of the liver is treifah and not from itself.) And Shach wrote in name of Maharil: if courtyard of liver punctured in place where butcher's hand rests — kosher; and if white threads like milk found around — old puncture and treifah — end; and see above siman 36 s.k. 5 in hagahah.`,
    '8#ב': `Kosher. And the reason is in Shach: since treifot is not from itself but that in the end liver will be removed. And accordingly whether membrane grew from blow or other membrane — it is sealing. And Taz wrote: even impure milk that generally does not seal — here in puncture of courtyard of liver it seals. Incident: lung of calf they tested omasum of liver and there was no flesh for courtyard of liver at all, only two skins exist one on another and flesh between them was entirely lacking; Bet Hillel ruled question 39 — treifah. Tzitz Eliezer: if needle stuck in rumen and opposite that place adhesion found — we attribute stringently, for certainly adhesion came from needle that punctured rumen through and through; but if needle or thorn placed so in rumen and not stuck, even if animal adhered somewhere — do not make treifah — see his name siman 49; and see Chokhmat Adam 69 and Peri Chadash who wrote he wrote well.`,
    '9#_': `In the omasum. Even if all hidden in omasum; and no distinction in omasum between kofa inward or outward. Shach. And Taz wrote examiner need not check courtyard of liver whether puncture; and though I saw in some examinations to check afterward — it appears not obligatory; and all the more one should not punish examiner for this — end.`,
    '10#א': `Livers. And Shach wrote: implies even if not adhered in place of vitality and place of gall — for any excess as stated, meaning as though both removed entirely; and so Tosafot Chadashim; and so Maharshal. However Maharshal wrote: if two livers begin below that olive in place of gall and place of vitality — obvious kosher according to everyone, like leg excess below knee (below siman 55 seif 4) — end; and one must say worse if excess of liver found not adhered at all to liver than if adhered below that olive, for since begins below from place of vitality and gall — appears only liver tendrils; and see end siman 43 and 54 and 55 what I wrote — end; and Taz wrote one does not forbid excess of liver except in place of gall or vitality.`,
    '10#ב': `In fowl. Goose liver divided into two parts — one large, one small; if small part removed and large remained — kosher in every case, for all are in large part: olive in place of gall, place depending on excess, place depending on kidneys; however if large part removed — see Mishnah Berurah siman 103; and so is practice daf 154. Liver that is swollen and thick and punctured — examine lung: if white like cotton found — treifah; if red — kosher, for it returns healthy; see Mishnah Berurah siman 21; and Darkei Moshe daf 95.`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `Liver removed — this treifah is from itself, for in it life depends; and the reason is not protection of the gall — Peri Chadash; and see Peleti where I elaborated on Tosafot and Rashba's words.`,
    '1#ב': `Place of its vitality — some say it depends on omasum, some on kidneys; therefore it suffices that the gemara requires two, which are three olive-volumes with olive in place of gall. And olive measure — be stringent that it must be half an egg; see Orach Chaim siman 486. And if membranes and sinews combine to olive — see Peleti where I elaborated; and Rashba and Tur should be relied upon that they combine.`,
    '1#ג': `Gathered together — all these are doubts and not definite treifah; therefore see Peleti: if olive in place depending on omasum was flattened and olive in place of kidneys whole — lenient from end siman 36.`,
    '2#_': `Even in ox — meaning every animal and beast, thin and thick are equal; only fowl is estimated according to value of animal; and measure according to value of small animal in small; and in fowl each goose — even if large and small goose — nevertheless all one measure; and see Peleti where Rosh and Tur disagree — all according to value and measure of olive in large depths — see at length.`,
    '3#א': `Liver dried — measure of its dryness as stated regarding lung; and simple.`,
    '3#ב': `These three olive-volumes — here one may be stringent and estimate per Rambam: olive is third of egg.`,
    '3#ג': `Even though remained — acharonim challenged Rama on this; and see Peleti I made somewhat similar; and be stringent when not place of loss.`,
    '3#ד': `Hard as stone — acharonim also challenged on this; see Peleti.`,
    '4#_': `Became wormy — acharonim raised: certainly must remain two olive-volumes without worm; only Gemara teaches us not to say throw rot in all of it.`,
    '6#א': `Thick side — Rashi and Tosafot method as mentioned in Shulchan Aruch; and Rashba's view is opposite; concern for both; if so law of kofa inward and outward in lung falls: wherever needle exits — treifah; and see Peleti siman 59 in their words; Rashba's words resolve well.`,
    '6#ב': `Faces to sides; and needle found in piece of liver after cooking — if woman did not deal with slaughter near cooking or at time of cooking — treifah; but if she dealt with it — kosher; and this appears the resolution between Tzitz Eliezer and Peri Chadash — what he wrote.`,
    '8#א': `From side of liver — some versions "from side of liver"; some "from side of liver below it" — not treifah; and all sealings help, even membrane from blow and impure milk — see Peleti what I wrote.`,
    '8#ב': `That certainly there is — Tzitz Eliezer's view: if needle stuck — adhesion is like puncture open through and treifah; but if no needle stuck or placed on spread — do not look at adhesion to make treifah; and see Peleti what I wrote, for perhaps one may side here where there are more sides to be lenient.`,
    '10#_': `Two livers — see what I wrote below siman 55 at length.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#א': `Liver removed, etc. — meaning of all poskim: liver removed from treifot from itself and not on account of gall; and so is meaning of Gemara; however Tosafot HaRishonim daf 43b in his challenge on Rambam who holds liver removed specifically is treifah and liver not created lacking — behold all liver treifot are on account of gall in creation and certainly treifah if punctured; and this teaches he holds liver treifot are on account of gall; and this is wondrous in Gemara that says it counts treifot from it but not from what is not from it; and see Rashi on one explanation on that which Rabbi Shimon bar Rabbi would immerse in ten, for he held soul depends on liver and life — see his name — that it is from itself. And it appears certainly according to all life depends on liver and soul, only it is not specified specifically in place of gall — why is place of gall stronger than other places? It is strained to say in place of gall life depends — this is possible to say regarding place of vitality, but regarding place of gall there is no reason life should depend there and soul dwells. (And in derush I said wittily regarding this dispute that came to Pumpedita: Rabbah and Rav Yosef fled ten; Rabbi Zeira met them and said: "Flee, olive that they said in place of gall!" We learned — what is this matter to your fleeing from the king's tax? But the matter according to simple sense: therefore we require olive in place of gall because there life of the body depends; and even though you have nothing more than gall, and all illness depends on gall and Satan, as he wished to afflict Job — gall was poured on his body, as written "He pours my gall on the ground"; nevertheless such is in its place of judgment — there He acted for good in the bad place the good always; and so His wisdom in good creation; and Moses buried in Peor, and so all — measure for measure. Behold they wished to flee not on account of tax as Tosafot wrote, but they did not wish to learn Torah in place of king's decree and Israel's distress; it was hard for them to learn Torah there. Rabbi Zeira said one should not do so; such is the measure as they said: from day Temple was destroyed it was decreed to learn Torah amid sorrow, poverty, and pressure; and the proof is the olive they said life of liver depends in place of gall — and this is simple.) Therefore Rashba certainly holds remnant required in liver is treifot from itself; and if no remnant like olive — behold treifah; but that it must be specifically place of gall and not another place — for this there is no reason of body's life; what is written "this place from that place" — only to protect the gall; and treifot depend on gall — and so on. And he argued well nevertheless on Rambam: if created lacking liver without its remnant — kosher to Rambam; and he challenged well: after all there is no protection for gall and gall is forbidden treifah — and so on; and clear. Peri Chadash wrote per Tosafot he has no proof that they stood on this; Rabbi Pinchas said liver removed and nothing remained — why needed? Without this treifah from removal of gall treifah; and answer: perhaps membrane remained in sinews and simfonot — end; and one may say: certainly if liver removed from illness — common that it is removed and melts in illness and sinews and simfonot remain; not so if created lacking: if by nature liver not created from six days of creation — simfonot would exist and be created — this thing is uncommon; if this is not — this too is not; if so Rashba who said in lacking liver — fine, there is no place for gall since simfonot do not exist; but Tosafot deal with removal that is common — one may say flesh of liver melted and simfonot remain and in them gall depends; and that Rashba did not challenge briefly on Rambam if liver lacking — perforce sinews lacking; and Rambam ruled simfonot of liver punctured — treifah; if so the same in lacking — one may say Rashba did not wish to hang this law on that law, for if we do not hold like Rambam in that — there would be no difficulty here; therefore Rashba challenged his difficulty from before; and difficulty in every case even if we do not hold like Rambam in that. But in truth anyway, as stated, Tosafot and Rashba appear divided in essence of law: for Rashba simfonot of liver and sinews are included in remnant; and that which teaches remnant like olive — sinews combine to olive. But Tosafot hold like Rambam they do not combine; if so anyway Rashba has no dependence on sinews as Tosafot wrote, for we learned nothing remained and even sinews do not exist; and see what I wrote above. But in essence of law, if liver treifot are on account of gall — Tosafot also appear to hold so, for why need Tosafot this strain that gall depends on simfonot and not explain simply therefore needed to teach liver removed even though gall removed — teaches even in species of animal and fowl that have no gall such as deer and doves — nevertheless treifah from liver removed; but per my words it is fine that Tosafot hold that which requires olive in place of gall is on account of gall; but species that have no gall — no olive needed in place of gall; if so, if mishnah that teaches nothing remained — deals with species that have no gall; and mishnah that teaches nothing remained like olive and Rashi explains like olive in place of gall — means they have gall and olive needed to protect; what does Gemara challenge mishnayot against each other and establish Rabbi Chiya and Rashba and not establish simply: first clause deals with species that have no gall and need no protection for gall, only liver matter alone suffices with any remnant; but second clause species that have gall and require olive remnant to protect gall, as stated; and this resolves. Perforce first clause also deals with species that have gall; and Tosafot's words fit well; and this requires study.`,
    '1#ב': `Olive-volume. Behold Rashba and Tur hold simfonot and sinews are included in remnant; and from Tosafot above it appears they hold not included — one may say it is a distant dispute and we do not act on it. Behold Rabbi Chiya holds any remnant helps; and Rashba requires some remnant — how say distant dispute to Rabbi Chiya any remnant helps even combining sinews and simfonot; to Rashba even flesh does not help until olive exists; and vice versa to Rashba how say simfonot do not combine to olive — for Rabbi Chiya we do not require olive at all and any remnant suffices. Therefore correct: for Rabbi Chiya any remnant of flesh suffices, not simfon; for Rashba olive remnant suffices combining simfonot too; and if so, if one simfon lacking and olive exists — kosher; and this is difficulty to Rambam who forbids from simfonot; and this requires study. Therefore for practical law: like Rashba and Tur — all combines; and we judge anonymous olive; and so is public custom.`,
    '1#ג': `Gathered or strap, etc. — forbidden; and in truth it should not have been clarified; and Rambam wrote: behold this is doubt and it appears to me forbidden; and reason needed why he wrote here "it appears to me" — is not every Torah doubt stringent? And it appears this regarding olive in place of gall and vitality they were stringent from doubt, since unknown which olive sages intended in mishnah; and this is: if flattened — is it remnant or not — also doubt; if so, if one of these two olive-volumes is flattened — there is double doubt lest olive not needed at all; and if you say required lest flattened be remnant (and unlike us who require three olive-volumes in place of gall, vitality in kidneys, omasum — not double doubt lest in place of gall or kidneys anyway on this olive only one doubt whether olive sages intended) — therefore Rambam wrote "appears"; or it is doubt of suspended; or since Gemara stated explicitly therefore we require two in place of gall and vitality — excluded from doubt and behold it is as certain; however here doubt whether olive in place depending on omasum or kidneys — fine, there would be double doubt.`,
    '2#_': `Even in large ox, etc. Chullin daf 44 s.v. kedei tefisat yad; and Tosafot on tefisat yad two explanations: Rashi four fingers; Bahag two fingers; afterward Tosafot brought Rivam: this is large ox; but in other animals, beasts, and fowl — estimate all according to value. Rabbenu Beit Yosef siman 20 above: Rivam spoke only per Rashi whose measures are large and impossible to say so in other animals; but per Bahag all animal and beast equal to large ox except fowl. And Rabbenu Beit Yosef learned this from Rambam and Rashba in Torat HaBayit daf 25: this measure of tefisat yad is for animal and beast; but in fowl according to value — namely poskim hold like Bahag two fingers not four; therefore no contradiction — Rashba daf 37 regarding tefisat yad at place of adhesion is tefisat yad — this measure for ox and small animal according to value of this measure; and seemingly contradiction — for above he wrote all animals equal; likewise daf 38 on two olive-volumes of liver: reasonable even in large ox and fowl all according to size; and what he said "even in large ox" implies other oxen and animals equal except fowl — they contradict. But in truth it resolves: where measure is large such as two fingers, and likewise liver (proper law only olive, but we are stringent from doubt to require two olive-volumes) — all animals that arise are equal; but where four fingers needed — not found in all arising animals — for all animals according to value; and what he wrote siman 46 on four fingers in large ox and small according to value — refers according to all that this is said in Gemara in shoe of ox and not said in animal — learn ox specifically; therefore difficulty for Rashba and Mechaber; therefore above siman 20 and everywhere Mechaber did not give measure according to value except in fowl. However Tur does not hold so: siman 20 after bringing Bahag who explained two fingers wrote: nevertheless Rivam's law — all according to value of large ox even animal; and Beit Yosef wondered; and indeed he followed Tosafot and Rosh who did not distinguish between Rashi and Bahag; also Rosh wrote explicitly regarding puncture like wing: obvious olive-volumes stated in fowl according to value, and in animal large and small estimate according to smallness as Rivam wrote regarding tefisat yad — end. If he held Rivam spoke only because four fingers required — how compare to liver and suffice with one olive that is only third of egg? Or he holds no distinction and not dependent on small and large measure; therefore Tur omitted "even in large ox" and wrote anonymously in fowl according to value — same in animal; only since Rosh wrote so in fowl he wrote also in fowl. If so, this law is disputed — be stringent. And I almost have proof for Rosh and Tur: if we say small calf and large ox — all measure two olive-volumes equal — then known: as animal grows so inner organs; if today liver lacking and only half olive remnant — treifah, and within eight days it grows according to animal value until half olive becomes full olive as way of large animals — used and kosher — we find treifah returns to kosher; impossible; and treifah does not improve; for this is not improvement but way of growth; if so treifah calf grazing in flock will not grow to ox — especially per those who say improves from doubt; perforce always estimate according to value; if so above case does not apply: necessarily according to value animal so treifot value; if then large was small — half olive liver called olive remnant also kosher; if grew afterward olive also grows; and this requires study. Perhaps included in what they said: whatever makes treifah within eight days does not improve; and so in liver.`,
    '3#א': `That in the end it will rot — all: Taz and other acharonim disagreed in this law whether from six days of creation to be concerned for this — only Rokeach deals when entire liver melted even two olive-volumes; but if two olive-volumes remain — kosher. I have nothing new except it is obligation to be beaten and kiss the dust — Rama. I said to jest somewhat: one must be precise in Gemara chapter "One who seized him": they said spitting blood — if lung disease — has remedy; if liver disease — no remedy; and Taz said reason: since from mouth — say perhaps melted and removed; and explanation needed: he said no remedy — do you decide it certainly to death? Then he said say perhaps melted — implies doubt whether melted; how decide certainly no remedy? Perforce intent: in the end there will be melting only for this we do not make treifah, for not yet melted; but this is only if there is limit that will stand; but all this time it will melt — if so, even though not yet melted, nevertheless it is as melted; and this is Gemara's intent "say perhaps melted" — every moment it was presumed to be melted.`,
    '3#ב': `Hard as stone treifah — Rav Beit Yosef doubted; and his intent: when we learn treifot one from another, and in Beit Yosef he learned law of dried that is also in its blemish animal — stated only: this case is different there air does not rule and returns healthy — we learn from lung that dried thus that crumbles under fingernail does not return healthy even if air does not rule; and this is principle everywhere. But dry as stone that was stated only regarding lung — how learn from it for us? If so, also in blemishes of firstborn: ear of animal hard as stone — disqualify like lung; for Gemara compares this more than liver; and all blemishes sages said in lung — also in liver should have taught; but specifically lung they said so, for all treifot as Rashi wrote: lest membrane puncture — this applies only in lung not liver; therefore in great loss certainly permit.`,
    '6#_': `And if thick side — Gemara says regarding needle found in piece of liver: we see if kofa outward — punctured; and similarly from needle in synagogue we do not distinguish kofa outward; Gemara answers there: there are foods and liquids that pressed it; Rashi explains difference: if thick head leans toward liver and point toward body cavity — how possible from outside and cavity it came? Then thick head would puncture liver — difficult in reality; we attribute via simfon entered — kosher. But opposite: perhaps thick leans toward body cavity — how via simfon puncture? Thick head not punctured; if so from body cavity punctured liver and via body cavity came via esophagus — treifah; this is Rashi's view and supporters; and this is Shulchan Aruch's view here; and Rashba and supporters opposite — we never concern needle came via body cavity, only via reed and simfon; only when point leans to reed and simfonot and thickness toward liver — even though pressed and entered inside liver flesh — nevertheless needle has no power to puncture in its travel and expulsion, for its thickness and pouch impede — therefore kosher; opposite — concern via travel it came in point and punctured simfon and similar slight puncture; Maharshal and more acharonim concerned for Rashba; if so law of Gemara distinction kofa inward and outward in lung falls, since Rashi and Rashba explain opposite to opposite and concern for both to be stringent. If needle swallowed in liver both heads thick and no point at all — per Rashba kosher, for how puncture diagonally and bent travel; but per Rashi treifah, for we attribute esophagus puncture; and if impossible to puncture — also in simfon impossible to puncture. Third method Rambam: he does not concern for esophagus or reed, only simfon swallowed inside liver also slight puncture; therefore in needle concern for simfon puncture; therefore if point toward animal cavity — treifah, simfon punctured and exited; but opposite kosher, for in pouch impossible puncture simfon, only exited via open mouth of simfon to liver — correct he explains outward and inward per Rashba. In Gemara difficulty from abomasum — Rashba explains even per Rambam: since you said in thick place hard to depict puncture and travel — if needle stuck in abomasum and pouch leans toward abomasum cavity — treifah, for perforce from outside point punctured and passed skin thickness until pouch exited outside toward cavity; if from face side outward — how could pouch puncture? Answer Gemara foods and liquids pressed. Peri Chadash challenged: Rambam explains in synagogue on one side — we puncture outer not inner; if so if questioner intent kofa toward synagogue cavity — treifah, for certainly punctured inside going outside; opposite how can pouch puncture? What does Gemara answer foods and liquids pressed? On contrary this supports passed inside to outside. See Peri Chadash explains dachahu — stranger they pushed needle backward — rejected, not seen. But appears certainly on one side kosher including all cases: either punctured from outside and did not reach abomasum cavity, or opposite punctured inside and did not reach animal cavity. Rashba wrote in section this needle, etc., to establish Rashi holds: if needle head lodged in liver goes to animal cavity — not in category needle in body cavity treifah, for they said only thorn that punctures with force from outside and enters with pressure; in this matter concern lest with pressured entrance it struck and touched slight puncture, etc.; per this needle from outside to body cavity lodged in organ like abomasum from outside — via pressure it lodged; if so no difficulty if kofa into synagogue cavity how puncture — entrance pressured as Rashba wrote they pressed perforce; and this resolves. Only Gemara difficulty opposite inner puncture not outer that entered via travel in synagogue — thorn via eating lodged while still fruit challenges; Gemara answers since there are foods, etc.; and this resolves.`,
    '8#א': `If omasum punctured, etc. — words of Bahag whose words are words of tradition; and one must be precise in Shulchan Aruch methods in Gemara that challenges daf 48: teach puncture of liver, puncture of wall, etc. — learn even treifot that are on account of another organ mishnah should count it — why no difficulty to Mar Ukva why not teach omasum punctured; and what is that reason because of lack of liver; strained to say since sealing helps anything — not counted. Therefore appears when we say treifah — per our establishment in liver we require complete remnant two olive-volumes; if so, in summer heat via courtyard of liver two olive-volumes will be consumed and measure reduced; but per mishnah tanna who holds any remnant suffices (and per most authors olive measure not mentioned in mishnah only baraita of Rabbi Chiya) — even if entire courtyard of liver punctured thus air does not rule to consume entirely without remnant — we are not concerned.`,
    '8#ב': `Certainly there is thorn or needle — in my presence in Prague it is very common in large animals there in that country to find in rumen, for maidservants putting feed for animals carry many needles on their heads and they fall into feed; when they come to innards they do so. Matter was hard for butchers and great loss; they instituted secret in elders' assembly: Rav Tzitz Eliezer holds even if not punctured through and through, adhesion that adhered to omasum is like puncture open through, for per many authors adhesion is like puncture in all organs; if so in such case for all like puncture open through; and he was precise: since he testified certainly needle exists — clear truth as is majority; if not found — lost without doubt in spread. If so, what need examination — certainly puncture here, and examination — what is its nature? Or adhesion worse than puncture open through since in other organs adhesion does not make treifah, and we say because needle began to puncture it swelled there in order of needle puncture, and from swelling and moisture omasum adhered — not decided needle passed through as puncture open through where we say if no blow — puncture open through from where; for adhesion in other organs comes from moisture and swelling; therefore commanded to check: if not punctured open through — do not make treifah; and this requires study; therefore where anyway sides to be lenient also on this we paid attention; broad-minded may decide; see responsum Chut HaShani that elaborated; requires study; but if anyway sides to permit — not heaven forbid to lenient always.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `That it depends on it. [Avnei Nezer; and see Peri Megadim in Sha'ar HaTzon s.k. 1 who wrote: accordingly three olives that fell into dish from three animals — one animal remained olive in place of omasum, one remained place of kidneys, one place of gall — need only 60 against one olive; and in drying all permitted, for perforce two kosher and one treifah and nullified in majority. And in opening to siman 98 I will explain whether in such case we say one in two nullifies, etc. — end. And in much — Rav's words are astonishing, for in such case on contrary perforce two treifot; and I thought printing error and where remained must say removed; but I saw in opening to laws of mixtures chapter 1 investigation 1 he mentioned both removed and remained: there one in two nullifies if specifically from permitted clarified initially, etc.; parable: three liver olives (not specifically) from three animals one removed place of vitality, one from gall, one from kidneys — if in each one olive remained, now perforce two of permitted, etc.; and likewise in flayed three animals one remained in navel, etc. — see his name; behold explicitly he equates remained to removed; and in truth this is not. Again I heard while genius author was alive a sage from community Slonim asked him this himself and he admitted to him a slip escaped his pen in this.]`,
    '2#_': `The two olives alone. See Turei Zahav who wrote language not precise; in truth even if one of them was consumed though all with second olive exists — Torah doubt, etc.; therefore for us who require three olives — if one dried or other flaw though two other olives with entire liver exist — treifah — end; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 2 who brought in name of his uncle genius author Panim Me'irot who wrote: specifically cited two olives; but in one of these not treifah — double doubt; and as Beit Yosef below siman 59 regarding flayed; and all the more here one may say perhaps law like Maharshal they brought; and he disagreed with Panim Me'irot and raised like Turei Zahav: even when one of these olives dried — treifah; but what Turei Zahav wrote at end regarding us who require three olives — in this he exaggerated to be stringent; and one may be lenient from double doubt — see his name — he raised so practically.`,
    '3#_': `To rot — all. Avnei Nezer in name of Taz and Shach; and see in this responsum his uncle Panim Me'irot part 1 siman 1; and see responsum Masat Binyamin siman 21 who also disagrees on Rama — see his name. [And in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 144 s.v. venava he wrote: in vain Mishnah Berurah challenged him; and worthy to be concerned for Rama's words — see his name.] Further he wrote there: also Rama was not stringent except in melting body from liver flesh; but if liver flesh exists and healthy only blood clotted and when scraping blood liver beneath exists — no concern at all even if liver flesh lacking measure of blood, etc. — see his name. And see responsum Geonei Batrai siman 12.`,
    '4#_': `Hard as stone. See She'elat Yaavetz part 2 siman 58 on hen that had large thick liver twice more than proper — he declared kosher unlike one sage who made treifah because swollen (called Geschwal in German) — no basis, for one does not add to treifot; and even if swelling also in place of two olives — kosher. He also wrote siman 59 on very fat rooster that had liver full hard white growths outside and inside, but all liver flesh was like nature showing other livers — he declared kosher — see his name.`,
    '5#_': `And in place of its vitality. [Avnei Nezer; and what he wrote agreement of Shach with Maharshal — see Shach where his agreement not found explicitly to make treifah; and in Peri Megadim wrote Shach permits if only two olives remained and became wormy, even by combination not two whole olives; and in truth also to permit his agreement not found explicitly; and so Peri Chadash wrote — see his name — Shach wavers in this law; and Peri Chadash's own view to make treifah if not like two whole olives aside from lack of worm punctures; and Turei Zahav was more stringent — see his name; and see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 44 who wrote: here in our community and in these regions very common in rams slaughtered by hundreds that their liver became wormy and literally many times not even two olives remain; they practiced leniency from old according to agreement of Shulchan Aruch and Shach; and though Rav author of She'erit Melamed was somewhat stringent — nevertheless I left them their custom — end; and requires study.]`,
    '6#_': `All buried. Avnei Nezer in incident they found, etc.; and see responsum Tur HaEven siman 17 section 30 who raised like Tzitz Eliezer kosher and not like Peri Chadash unless recognizable it was made while alive — see his name. [And so leans to be lenient in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 43 he brought in siman 40 s.k. 1; and Turei Zahav's view to forbid only if needle stuck on liver and not inside and no rust — permit — see his name. And see Sefer Levushei Serad siman 22: incident came before him — man bought liver piece from butcher, left in house, after two or three hours found nail stuck in one simfon, thick side of nail toward animal cavity and point toward animal's head; buyer brought to butcher who answered he knows not, only somewhat wonder in his eyes if from beginning how did he not feel. That nail had no rust; in buyer's house such nails found for sale. He ruled animal permitted but Reuven buyer forbidden to eat from it and that liver piece forbidden everyone — see his name with explanation. And see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 46 incident goose slaughtered, esophagus checked as usual; next day intestines removed with liver, placed on table; after hour when took liver to rinse in water found golden grain half small bean, unknown from where, no mark anywhere, no rust on gold, no blow on liver or organ. He wrote simple to permit: servant said this is place reticulum placed on liver when esophagus torn from there; one may say fat from esophagus in life swallowed it and at tearing fell there, or when reticulum removed this exited and fell on liver; simple we attribute: even if esophagus before us and punctured and golden grain beneath in way we know certainly exited from esophagus and punctured esophagus before us — we would attribute butcher's touch as all treifot; no distinction because esophagus doubtful nevelah and doubtful slaughter. This is not called doubtful slaughter but doubtful in act of slaughter such as doubt it delayed, trampled, damaged knife, slaughtered esophagus and grain found loose — nothing to attribute; and so Shach end siman 34 (already elaborated more siman 24 and 25 there and brought somewhat above siman 33 s.k. 4 in s.v. aval). And since so — likewise since can attribute here esophagus torn when reticulum torn from it, etc. — and this a fortiori from what Turei Zahav wrote if needle stuck on liver, etc. — all the more here can attribute more and only placed on liver not inside at all — permit — see his name.] And see responsum Tiferet Tzvi part 14 siman 6 who debated this law.`,
    '7#_': `In the liver. Avnei Nezer; and see responsum Shemesh Tzedakah part 4 siman 41.`,
    '8#_': `If omasum punctured. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second edition part Yoreh De'ah siman 12 s.v. ve'al hanefesh who wrote: if created thus with puncture — per his view kosher — see his name.`,
    '9#_': `From side of liver treifah. Avnei Nezer; and see responsum Givat Shaul siman 55: in courtyard of liver was round puncture and lip around puncture in upper skin toward lung side; also flesh between two skins lacking in circle like skin; recognizable made while alive; in lower skin toward liver no such lack, only torn to several tears; slaughterer said lower skin stuck to rumen and torn from there with force; this puncture was not toward liver side — he wrote to permit for several reasons — see his name.`,
    '10#_': `But if it adhered. See responsum Mahari Trani part 2 siman 1.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `A) [Seif 1] Liver removed — treifah; even if gall remained stuck in sinews of liver and simfonot — treifah. Tosafot Chullin 42a s.v. nikav. Peri Chadash ot 1; and he wrote: not like Rashba in long Tosafot 43b who wrote no prohibition of liver removal except from lack of gall that does not exist. Per this Orach Chaim ot 1; Likutei HaRambam ot 1; Kereti ot 1; Turei Zahav ot 1; Sha'ar HaTziyon ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; Chokhmat Adam general rule 17 ot 9; Beit Yitzchak ot 1; Minchat Yitzchak in roots ot 1; Zivchei Tzvi ot 1.`,
    '2#_': `B) There. Unless olive remained in place of gall, etc. Explanation: place where it rooted and hung in liver as explained Rashi Chullin 46a end s.v. matbil bah; and so in explanation not like Rabbi Azriel siman 371 who explained olive in place of gall means opposite gall — this explanation not brought in any posek. From this Orach Chaim ot 3; and in his book Gidulei HaKodesh ot 1; Zivchei Tzvi ot 2.`,
    '3#_': `C) There. Unless olive in place of gall and olive in place of its vitality that depends on it. Namely place of its hanging when exposed and stuck under kidneys — Rashi Chullin 46a; and Rashba and Rambam chapter 8 Hilchot Shechitah; and Beit Yosef wrote and so is essence; therefore ruled so in Shulchan Aruch. And Rivan and Rokeach wrote: in place where it is alive — namely place of hanging in omasum; and so Rashi there in foreign tongue; and Beit David Beit Yosef. Therefore Orach Chaim siman 51 ot 8 wrote: need three olives in these three places — meaning olive in place of gall, olive in place stuck under kidneys, olive in place of hanging in omasum. And Beit David Rama in his book Tosafot Chadashim general rule 89 ot 9 section ot 2; Taz s.k. 1; Shach s.k. 1; so is practice in Hagahot Beit Yosef ot 3; Peri Chadash ot 2; for this Orach Chaim ot 2; Kereti ot 2; Turei Zahav ot 3; Sha'ar HaTziyon ot 3; Zivchei Tzedek ot 3. Machatzit HaShekel ot 5; Chokhmat Adam there; Anaf Yosef siman 30 ot 1; Tosafot Zahav siman 65 ot 2; Beit Yitzchak ot 1; Kometz general rule 5 laws of other treifot ot 3; Zivchei Tzvi ot 3.`,
    '4#_': `D) However in financial loss or for honor of Shabbat — if olive in place of gall and olive in place of hanging in omasum — one may declare kosher. Masat Binyamin responsum 103; so is practice there; Minchat Yitzchak on Tosafot Chadashim there ot 25; and so in his book Shevut Yaakov part 1 siman 51. But Machatzit HaShekel there wrote: in financial loss or Shabbat honor if olive in place of gall and olive in place of kidneys — declare kosher per Shulchan Aruch ruling; and wrote: though Moharach in Tosafot Chadashim brought Orach Chaim's words requiring three olives — here in hagahah he retracted since he did not emend Shulchan Aruch's words. And so Anaf Yosef there: in financial loss be lenient if remained olive in place of gall and olive in place of kidneys to rely on Shulchan Aruch ruling. Zivchei Tzvi ot 4; and he wrote: but if only olive in place of gall and olive in place of omasum — make treifah — end; and so appears from view of Rashi, Rambam, Rashba, Shulchan Aruch, and Rama who did not emend Shulchan Aruch.`,
    '5#_': `E) And he wrote there Sha'ar HaTziyon: this requiring three olives is from doubt; accordingly three olives that fell into one dish from three animals — one remained olive in place of omasum, one in kidneys, one in gall — need only 60 against one olive; and in drying all permitted, for perforce two kosher and one treifah and nullified in majority. And Hagahat HaPanim ot 1; and wondered on him: in such case on contrary perforce two treifot; see his name; and Mekor Chaim ot 5 wrote what Sha'ar HaTziyon wrote "remained" is scribal error and must say removed — meaning one removed olive of omasum, one place of kidneys, one place of gall — need 60 only against one of them, for two of them kosher and we do not know which — end.`,
    '6#_': `F) There. And these two olives shall each be whole in its place; but if gathered, strap, or flattened — treifah. And likewise three olives we wrote above ot 3 — must not be gathered, flattened, or strap. Orach Chaim there. And if olive of excess or olive of kidneys flattened, etc., and behold two whole olives — declare kosher from double doubt. And whatever has olive of gall and another whole olive — even if third flattened whether in omasum or kidneys — kosher. Machatzit HaShekel ot 7; and he wrote: so proven from end of Peleti ot 3; and so is essence; not like what he wrote: if olive in place of omasum flattened — see his name. Anaf Yosef siman 30 ot 5; Tosafot Zahav siman 65 ot 4; Beit Yitzchak in Tosafot HaRishonim section ot 1; Zivchei Shaul ot 5; Zivchei Tzvi ot 7; and implies always olive in place of gall required, only regarding whole olive and flattened olive no distinction whether whole in kidneys and flattened in omasum or opposite. And see below ot 14.`,
    '7#_': `G) There. But if gathered or strap, etc. — not specifically these forms only, but anything not whole as created — olive in those places; even if all rest whole — treifah. Turei Zahav ot 4; Beit Yitzchak in Amud HaYemini ot 3; Anaf Yosef there ot 2; and so Shach s.k. 3: if these two olives removed even though entire liver remains — treifah. Sha'ar HaTziyon end ot 3; Machatzit HaShekel end ot 14; Chokhmat Adam there ot 9; Anaf Yosef there ot 9; Zivchei Tzvi ot 8.`,
    '8#_': `H) These olives — Kohen Perot ot 2 wrote flesh and sinews and simfonot combine to olive measure; and so is public custom; but Turei Zahav ot 2 wrote they do not combine; must be all olives from liver flesh alone. And so Sha'ar HaTziyon ot 3; Anaf Yosef there ot 4; Zivchei Tzvi ot 9; and see below ot 14.`,
    '9#_': `I) And olive measure — some say half egg, namely nine dirhams; some third egg, namely six dirhams. Sha'arei Teshuvah ot 3; and be stringent that it must be half egg. Kereti ot 2; Chokhmat Adam there. And this stringency for stringency such as liver removed and only olives remain; but for leniency such as liver exists and only olives removed — not half-egg measure, for that is leniency; rather since removed from them third-of-egg measure — forbidden. Sha'ar HaTziyon ot 1; Beit Yitzchak in Tosafot HaRishonim ot 1; Kometz laws of other treifot general rule 5 ot 4; Zivchei Tzvi ot 11. And so we wrote in Orach Chaim siman 486 ot 1 in name of several poskim: olive measure as half egg — to be stringent not to be lenient — see his name.`,
    '10#_': `J) And it appears if third-of-egg measure exists from flesh alone and completion of half egg from sinews and simfonot — kosher, for double doubt in dispute of rabbis. And appears further: if fed in place of gall and olive in place hanging on kidneys — whole from flesh alone and half-egg measure for each; and third olive for omasum — its measure third of egg; and with sinews — kosher even without financial loss from reason I wrote; and likewise opposite. Anaf Yosef there. And his intent in "and likewise opposite": if olive in place of gall and olive in omasum whole in flesh alone and half egg for each; and third olive hanging on kidneys — third egg with sinews complete half egg — kosher even without financial loss; but if olive in place hanging on kidneys and olive of omasum whole from flesh alone and half egg each and third olive in place of gall — third egg and sinews do not permit; for as written "and likewise opposite" intent specifically regarding olive of omasum and olive of kidneys; but olive in place of gall — no permission; for so we find in law of flattened — need whole olive in place of gall and another with it; but if two olives of omasum and kidneys whole and olive in place of gall flattened — no permission as Machatzit HaShekel ot 7 — see his name. But it appears even olive in place of gall — third egg and sinews complete half egg — may declare kosher from above reason that flattened law is different, for Gemara asked even on one olive as Machatzit HaShekel wrote; but regarding half egg and sinews — dispute of rabbis — we do double doubt even on olive in place of gall. Zivchei Tzvi ot 12.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 41 seif 1) Liver removed unless olive remained, etc. Know statement of Rabbi Yitzchak bar Yosef who said Rabbi Yochanan in Piskei Avodah Zarah (daf 43): and so meaning of sugya (daf 46); and as Tosafot there wrote. But in mishnah of Rabbi Pinchas: liver removed and nothing remained from it — implies if anything remained — kosher and we do not require olive. And in Gemara there they challenge each other: in mishnah of "these are kosher" (daf 54) it teaches liver removed and olive remained from it; and they answer: this Rabbi Shimon, this Rabbi Chiya; and in Gemara there they challenge Rabbi Yochanan who ruled olive required, yet Rav Bibi said Rabbi Yochanan: law follows anonymous mishnah. And Rashi: what strength has latter anonymous against former anonymous? They answer: two tannaim according to Rabbi Yochanan. From all these it is proven we read in mishnah of "these are kosher" — olive. And Rif and Ran read in mishnayot of "these are kosher" — any amount. And Maharam of Rothenburg wondered on them in gloss of Rif — see his name. It escaped him Rashba's words in Tosafot (daf 39) who challenged this version and read in mishnah of "these are kosher" — any amount; and mishnayot do not contradict. And Gemara challenges from baraita that teaches olive required; answers: this Rabbi Shimon, etc.; and therefore challenges Rabbi Yochanan how ruled baraita and abandoned mishnah, yet Rav Bibi said law follows anonymous mishnah; answers two tannaim according to Rabbi Yochanan; and clear this is Rif and Ran's version. For practical law certainly no difference, for certainly olive required in place of gall, etc., as Shulchan Aruch wrote. Know: liver removed treifah even in species that have no gall in liver and place of their remnant equal; and see acharonim siman 42 s.k. 12; and so proven explicitly in Tosafot Kiddushin (daf 57) s.v. shechatah, etc., who wrote regarding bird of metzora found treifah in intestines such as liver removed — see his name. Bird of metzora was turtle-dove as in Negaim chapter 14; Taz brings siman 82. And turtle-dove has no gall in liver as acharonim wrote in that s.k., for liver removed is not treifah from lack of gall, since possible all liver flesh removed and gall stuck in liver sinew as Tosafot beginning Avodah Zarah; and not as appears from Rashba in Tosafot (daf 43) whom Beit Yosef brings at end of this siman that liver removed — we come on account of lack of gall. And this is astonishing: how hang baraita on what baraita did not teach — this is explicit mishnah liver removed and nothing remained — treifah, and none disagree; and if gall lacking — majority of poskim permit as explained Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, Shach end siman 42; Rashba's words require study. And it does not appear so in any posek, for even in species without gall in liver — treifah if their liver removed, as written; and see Turei Zahav. And if liver lacking from start of its creation — explained Beit Yosef at end of this siman and Shulchan Aruch siman 50 s.k. 3: for Rashba and Ran treifah; for Rambam kosher. However Kohen Perot wrote: even for Rambam one may say treifah. And in Tosafot Kiddushin explained explicitly treifah, for they wrote found treifah in intestines such as liver removed — certainly from birth. And since Tosafot's view is to be stringent — do not be lenient, for Rambam is lone against Tosafot, Rashba, and Ran; and not like hagahah siman 50 s.k. 3 that ruled lenient in financial loss. And so Turei Zahav and Peri Chadash to be stringent even in financial loss — see his name. It escaped him Tosafot's view here — see his name.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `In place of its vitality that it depends. See Avnei Nezer; and what he wrote in name of Shevut Yaakov siman 51 — see his name — who wrote: one year most animals found liver rotted; and see Machatzit HaShekel who brought responsa Maharam Zacut: if bubbles in liver or tanner in place of gall or vitality — declare kosher; and all the more this is financial loss as many livers full of bubbles; and what they practiced to make treifah thinking this prohibition — not custom; and so agreed Maharash Abuhav and all yeshiva rabbis, etc.; however Gur Aryeh Levi was uncertain about tanner in place of gall and vitality — see his name; and see in Sulam LaMinchah end general rule 89.`,
    '2#_': `That in the end it will rot — all. Avnei Nezer on melting liver and two olive-volumes remained — all acharonim permit; and Machatzit HaShekel among permitters, for they are many; and he raised: so we cite and not like Beit Levi — see his name; and see his name brought She'elat Yaavetz siman 58 part 2 on large liver; also found swollen — kosher, not worse than melting; and not like sage who made treifah — see his name; and see end siman 36 that Avnei Nezer brought one must examine lung, etc.; and I wrote in my book part 1 — I will bring words of genius author of Noda BiYehudah in his gloss 14 — and see what he wrote there; and see responsum Bach Chadashot from son of Bach to answer difficulties of one sage who challenged Bach on melting liver; sage's difficulty is in responsum Geonei Batrai siman 12; and see in Peri Megadim on geese that are fattened — common illness of liver melting; and though no power to forbid others — one may rule permit when two whole olive-volumes remain as Mishnah Berurah and other acharonim; nevertheless ba'al nefesh and ba'al Torah should be stringent for himself to be concerned for words of geonim who are stringent — see his name.`,
    '3#_': `Needle found in liver. See in Sefer HaMakhria daf 15 responsum Shemesh Tzedakah siman 41; and in my book part 1 in pamphlet of answers at length on this in Shulchan Aruch seif 10; and see in responsum Noda BiYehudah end siman 5 who elaborated to be stringent and make treifah in financial loss.`,
    '4#_': `With adhesion on rib — kosher. Avnei Nezer; and see in my book pamphlet of answers siman 30 and 31 I elaborated law of adhesion from omasum to synagogue; and found needle; and I raised: if stuck in walls of abomasum and synagogue lengthwise and all hidden within thickness of wall — make treifah even if no adhesion opposite; and all the more when adhesion opposite; even in financial loss. And where not stuck inside wall only placed lengthwise between folds of abomasum and needle point not opposite wall and adhesion outside where needle mouth ended — hard in my eyes to permit. But since usual and very common and mostly financial loss and honor of Shabbat — it appeared in my humble opinion to be lenient if adhesion passes via narrowing and actual touch, not via peeling with fingernail, only roll between fingers and gently feel adhesion. (And though perhaps they did not accept narrowing matter only in lung — if adhesion all day will not disconnect unlike other organs where adhesion disconnects, as written regarding calves; but it appears whatever said adhesion in organs — word applies also to narrowing — their law like lung and enough; and not like calves where from reasoning so, not so here that at least legs to matter — not from puncture since disconnected with light touch — great doubt to permit.) And so appears to be lenient when see flesh in omasum of liver at adhesion place is flawed like blow — then attribute flaw to flawed place, namely adhesion came from omasum; and in abomasum no consumption, etc. — see his name; and see his name I brought responsum Noda BiYehudah siman 15 and see his name siman 18. And if puncture open through or adhesion requiring check — if checked in spread and no needle found at all — no concern lest lost on account of majority of spread — see his name; and see Peri Megadim part 2 siman 129 on what Chut HaShani wrote to be lenient without checking spread — heaven forbid to practice so, clarified in many experiments it is very common. And if impossible to check all belly rooms — make treifah; and so I heard Ashkenaz practice; and in my opinion one who does not practice so feeds treifot — see his name; and he wrote in Noda BiYehudah siman 16: adhesion from omasum to synagogue — already discarded rumen or cut and salted and rinsed in way impossible to search for needle except woman says to her son there was no needle — make treifah, for since she did not yet know from adhesion — it is matter not dependent, etc. — see his name.`,
  },
};
